{"content": "## Example API Call to Create a Google Cloud Compute Engine Instance with NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU (if available) and Ubuntu 20.04 LTS

> **Note**: As of the latest Google Cloud documentation, the NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPU is not listed among the supported GPU types for Compute Engine. The closest available GPU is the NVIDIA Tesla A100. If you specifically need an RTX A6000, you may need to check with Google Cloud support or use a custom image with the required drivers.

Below is a REST API example that demonstrates how to create an instance with a GPU (replace `nvidia-tesla-a100` with your desired GPU type if RTX A6000 becomes available). The example uses the Compute Engine API v1.

```json
POST https://compute.googleapis.com/compute/v1/projects/{PROJECT_ID}/zones/{ZONE}/instances
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {ACCESS_TOKEN}

{
  "name": "my-rtx-a6000-instance",
  "machineType": "zones/{ZONE}/machineTypes/n1-standard-8",
  "disks": [
    {
      "boot": true,
      "autoDelete": true,
      "initializeParams": {
        "sourceImage": "projects/ubuntu-os-cloud/global/images/ubuntu-2004-focal-v20230912"
      }
    }
  ],
  "networkInterfaces": [
    {
      "network": "global/networks/default",
      "accessConfigs": [
        {
          "type": "ONE_TO_ONE_NAT",
          "name": "External NAT"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "guestAccelerators": [
    {
      "acceleratorType": "projects/{PROJECT_ID}/zones/{ZONE}/acceleratorTypes/nvidia-tesla-a100",
      "acceleratorCount": 1
    }
  ],
  "serviceAccounts": [
    {
      "email": "default",
      "scopes": [
        "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "metadata": {
    "items": [
      {
        "key": "startup-script",
        "value": "#!/bin/bash\napt-get update\napt-get install -y nvidia-driver-535"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

### Steps to Execute
1. **Obtain an OAuth 2.0 access token** with the scope `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform`. You can use `gcloud auth application-default print-access-token` or any other method.
2. Replace `{PROJECT_ID}`, `{ZONE}`, and `{ACCESS_TOKEN}` with your actual project ID, zone (e.g., `us-central1-a`), and access token.
3. Send the POST request using `curl`, Postman, or any HTTP client.

#### Example `curl` Command
```bash
curl -X POST \
  "https://compute.googleapis.com/compute/v1/projects/${PROJECT_ID}/zones/${ZONE}/instances" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ${ACCESS_TOKEN}" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d @instance-config.json
```

Replace `@instance-config.json` with the path to a file containing the JSON body above.

### Using `gcloud` CLI (Alternative)
If you prefer the CLI, you can create a similar instance with:

```bash
gcloud compute instances create my-rtx-a6000-instance \
  --project=${PROJECT_ID} \
  --zone=${ZONE} \
  --machine-type=n1-standard-8 \
  --image-project=ubuntu-os-cloud \
  --image=ubuntu-2004-focal-v20230912 \
  --accelerator=type=nvidia-tesla-a100,count=1 \
  --metadata startup-script='#!/bin/bash\napt-get update\napt-get install -y nvidia-driver-535'
```

> **Tip**: Always verify the GPU type availability in your chosen zone via the Google Cloud Console or the `gcloud compute accelerator-types list` command.

---

**Disclaimer**: This example assumes you have the necessary IAM permissions to create instances and attach GPUs in the specified project and zone. Ensure your billing account is active and has GPU usage enabled.
", "filename": "google-cloud-create-instance.md"}

